  "apod": {
    "date": "2026-11-17",
    "title": "Milky Way versus Aurora",
    "explanation": " Explanation:  Are these two giant arches battling for control of the sky? No, but it sure looks like it! The arch on the left is the central band of our  Milky Way Galaxy , an ancient system of stars, gas, and dust that predates  our Earth  and is very far away. The arch on the right, by contrast, is an  auroral ribbon  that is only hours old, composed of energized  atoms and molecules , and is close by -- right in  Earth's upper atmosphere . These chance-superposed  titan s have  our Sun  in common, a  stellar member  of the Milky Way that ejects energetic particles which create  aurora s. Far behind the middle of the  auroral arch  is the  Big Dipper . Even farther in the distance is the  Andromeda galaxy  -- the bright spot near the top of the frame, just left of center. The  featured image  was taken about a month ago from  Kvaløya  in northern  Norway .   Tomorrow's picture:  open space",
    "image": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/GalaxyVsAurora_Scheib_960.jpg/jcr:content/renditions/cq5dam.web.1280.1280.jpeg",
    "link": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-17-milky-way-versus-aurora/",
    "credit": " Guillaume Scheib ",
    "alt": "A starry night sky is seen above water and some islands. In the night sky are two bright arches that are nearly vertical. On the left is the textured Milky Way and on the right is a  smoother ribbon of aurora. Please see the explanation for more detailed information.",
    "media_type": "image"
  },
  "updated": "2026-11-17T10:09:02.560892+00:00"
